      "title": "Fruit, flies and getting frisky: how food odours trigger sexual arousal",
      "link": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03271-0",
      "description": "<p>Nature, Published online: 04 November 2026; <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03271-0\">doi:10.1038/d41586-026-03271-0</a></p>Males of a Drosophila species that thrives on a particular fruit need the smell of food to begin courtship. This environmental cue acts on defined mating circuits in the brain.",
      "source_name": "Nature Latest Research",
      "published": "2026-11-04T00:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "Ecological and social cues orchestrate concerted courtship in <i>Drosophila</i>",
      "link": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11209-9",
      "description": "<p>Nature, Published online: 04 November 2026; <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11209-9\">doi:10.1038/s41586-026-11209-9</a></p>In Drosophila erecta, food odours initiate male sexual arousal, which is subsequently reinforced by the visual motion of other courting flies, creating a multisensory mechanism that synchronizes courtship in groups when conditions are favourable for reproduction.",
      "source_name": "Nature Latest Research",
      "published": "2026-11-04T00:00:00"
